  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a license to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S4534",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4534-2013",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4534\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in\nrelation to a license to sell liquor at retail for consumption on\ncertain premises\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: This bill would allow the Afrikan\nCultural and Caterers Center, Inc. (Afrika House) to obtain a liquor\nlicense.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: This bill amends the Alcoholic\nBeverage Control Law, allowing issuance of a retail license for\non-premises consumption of alcohol for a premises within 200 feet of a\nplace of worship, provided that the purpose of such premises is for\nthe sale and consumption of food or beverage, and such premises is\nwithin the boundaries of the borough of Brooklyn as directed therein.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: While the Alcoholic Beverage Control.  Law prohibits\nthe sale for or-premises consumption of alcohol at any location within\n200 feat of a place of worship, exceptions have been made for certain\nestablishments that do not pose problems for the place of worship, or\nthe surrounding neighborhood. This bill would exempt the Afrikan\nCultural and Caterers Center, Inc. (Afrika House) in New York City\nfrom this provision of law, allowing sale and consumption of alcohol\nin the banquet halls.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  2011-2012 A.4371 2009-2010 A.3555A\n2006-2009 A.5009 2004-2005 A.4319\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
